        "Dimitri and I spent the following day planning the response, not in anger — anger clouds precision, and precision is the only thing that has ever kept this organization alive against families with three times our numbers — but with the specific cold clarity of men who understand that a single clean strike, correctly placed, is worth more than a hundred sloppy ones.",
        "\"He'll have security layered deep,\" Dimitri said, spreading a map of the eastern route across my desk, marking positions with quick, economical strokes. \"Even arrogant, he won't be careless. We're looking at a minimum of twelve men on rotation, probably closer to eighteen given the value of the cargo he thinks he's stealing.\"",
        "\"We're not fighting eighteen men.\" I traced the route with one finger, found the bend in the road two kilometers before the interception point, a stretch bordered by dense treeline on both sides, the kind of terrain that turns a convoy's numerical advantage into a liability. \"We're removing one. Everything else is noise.\"",
        "We moved before dawn, six men including myself, positioned in the treeline four hours ahead of the convoy's expected arrival, radios silent, communication reduced to a system of signals we'd used together for years. I want to be honest about the waiting — four hours of total stillness, no food, no unnecessary movement, breath controlled, mind narrowed to a single point of focus, is its own particular discipline, one most men in this business never fully master. I have always been good at it. Stillness has never frightened me the way it seems to frighten other men, men who need constant motion to avoid sitting too long with their own thoughts.",
        "The convoy arrived twenty minutes behind schedule, six vehicles, exactly the numbers Sergei had confirmed under interrogation. Marco Castellano rode in the third vehicle, three cars back from the front line, close enough to observe the ambush he believed he was springing on us, far enough back that he clearly believed himself insulated from any actual danger.",
        "He was wrong about that. Arrogance, I've found, is always the most reliable weakness to exploit in men who've inherited power rather than built it themselves."
